I ruled on pool-closing-2 and committed the spec change at `a3551571`. I skipped the optional `jev cite` check and the commands written in the reply were the only verification. The commit and ruling file were made, but the full output of `git commit` and of the `$` lines was never displayed.

The ruling follows option A from the question: closing stays on by default and no assertion is loosened.

- **The rule 16 tests** (`funded.spec.ts` and the first `policy.spec.ts` case) check that an application waits on a spent pool. The spec keeps that behaviour only with `poolClosing: false`, so they run with it off and keep their assertions.
- **The other three cases** (the random-finances case in `policy.spec.ts`, `retained.spec.ts` and `apply.spec.ts`) test serving, re-making and caching against the base timeline, not closing. They also run with `poolClosing: false` and keep their assertions. Closing is covered in `closing.spec.ts`.
- **The `pools.spec.ts` guard** still forbids serving and arrivals from reading `pools[q].inflows`. It now allows one read: the closing check's `lastArrivalOf` reading the frame definition's `inflows`, which the spec requires.

I took the question's account of the six failures from the implementer's report and did not re-run anything. That includes the claim that the per-timeline cache bound still holds in `retained.spec.ts`.

The edits are all in `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-22-pool-closing-design.html`, in the option section, the changed-files list (which now names the five spec files) and the other-tests section. No interface row changed. The only unit affected is pool-closing, as a clarification, so its Task 3 work still holds and it can commit.

